      "text": "To determine the number that is a multiple of every integer, we need to understand the concept of multiples and the properties of integers. \n\n1. **Definition of a Multiple**:\n   - A multiple of an integer \\( n \\) is any number that can be expressed in the form \\( k \\cdot n \\) where \\( k \\) is an integer.\n   - For example, the multiples of 5 are \\( 5, 10, 15, 20, \\ldots \\).\n\n2. **Finding the Least Common Multiple (LCM)**:\n   - The least common multiple of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the integers in the set.\n   - For example, the LCM of 2, 3, and 4 is 12 because 12 is the smallest number that is divisible by 2, 3, and 4.\n\n3. **Nature of the LCM**:\n   - The LCM of any set of integers is a positive integer.\n   - It is the smallest number that can be divided by each number in the set without leaving a remainder.\n\n4. ** multiples of every integer**:\n   - Consider the product of all positive integers: \\( 1, 2, 3, 4, \\ldots \\).\n   - For any positive integer \\( k \\), the product \\( 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot k \\) is a multiple of \\( k \\).\n   - This is because every integer \\( k \\) can be expressed as a product of primes in the same manner as any other integer, and the product of all these primes will always include \\( k \\) as a factor.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The product of all positive integers is a number that is divisible by every positive integer.\n   - This number is denoted as \\( \\infty \\), but in a mathematical context, we say it is the product of all prime numbers.\n\nTherefore, the number that is a multiple of every integer is the product of all positive integers, which is often denoted as \\( \\infty \\). However, in terms of a finite product, the answer can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nThis is a theoretical answer since the concept of multiplying all positive integers directly does not result in a finite number. Instead, it represents an infinite product.",
